# Rational Fabrication of Low-Coordinate Single-Atom Ni Electrocatalysts by MOFs for Highly Selective CO2 Reduction

## abstract
Single-atom catalysts (SACs) have attracted tremendous interests due to their ultrahigh activity and selectivity. However, the rational control over coordination microenvironment of SACs remains a grand challenge. Herein, a post-synthetic metal substitution (PSMS) strategy has been developed to fabricate single-atom Ni catalysts with different N coordination numbers (denoted Ni-Nx -C) on pre-designed N-doped carbon derived from metal-organic frameworks. When served for CO2 electroreduction, the obtained Ni-N3 -C catalyst achieves CO Faradaic efficiency (FE) up to 95.6 %, much superior to that of Ni-N4 -C. Theoretical calculations reveal that the lower Ni coordination number in Ni-N3 -C can significantly enhance COOH* formation, thereby accelerating CO2 reduction. In addition, Ni-N3 -C shows excellent performance in Zn-CO2 battery with ultrahigh CO FE and excellent stability. This work opens up a new and general avenue to coordination microenvironment modulation (MEM) of SACs for CO2 utilization.

Rational Fabrication of Low-Coordinate Single-Atom Ni Electrocatalysts by MOFs for Highly Selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Reduction
Single-atom catalysts (SACs) with maximized utilization of metal atoms have captured widespread interest in heterogeneous catalysis.[1] Given that single metal atoms are stabilized by the supports based on coordination interaction, the catalytic performance of SACs is highly sensitive to their local coordination environment from the viewpoint of coordination chemistry.[1c,2] On account of this, great efforts have been devoted to the coordination environment regulation of SACs.[1c,2] The coordination number of SACs, an important parameter of coordination microenvironment modulation (MEM), would greatly influence the electronic and geometric structure of metal centers in SACs, playing significant roles in catalytic activity and selectivity.[2c,3] Specifically, in single metal atoms incorporated N-doped carbon materials $(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{1}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N-}\mathbf{C})$ , the number variation of coordinated $\mathbf{N}$ atoms in SACs can induce differentiated local electronic densities of central metal atoms, which greatly affects the adsorption of reaction intermediates and modulates the catalytic activity of SACs.[4] Although significant progress has been achieved in the construction of SACs, there is lack of effective strategy to precisely regulate coordination number without disturbing the single-atom dispersion of metal centers.[2c,3a,d, 5]
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs),[6] as a class of crystalline porous materials featuring tailorable composition and structure, have demonstrated particular advantages on the accurate construction of efficient catalysts.[7] Moreover, MOFs have been recognized to be one of the most promising candidates toward precise regulation on the coordination environment of SACs.[4,8] While great efforts have been devoted, the currently adopted strategies to the modulation of coordination number are almost limited to the one-step pyrolysis of targeted metal-doped MOF precursors, where the higher temperature leads to lower $\mathbf{N}$ coordination number.[4c,5a] However, the doped metal species possibly disturbs the MOF formation. Accordingly, the synthesis of metal doped MOF must be modified according to doped metal species.[4a,c,8c] Meanwhile, given the synchronous MOF carbonization and single atom formation, complex synthetic parameters, such as pyrolysis temperature, metal loading, etc., interplay each other in the one-step pyrolysis, seriously restricting the flexibility and universality of this strategy.[4a,c,8c] On account of the dilemma above, the development of more general approaches is highly desired to achieve the accurate control over coordination environment in MOF-based SACs. The decoupling of MOF carbonization and single atom decoration, which can avert complex variates in the previous one-step pyrolysis method, might be a promising and easy-tohandle solution to coordination microenvironment modulation of SACs.
The $Z{\mathrm{n-MOF}}.$ , named ZIF-8 or MAF-4,[9] in a dodecahedral morphology with a size of ${\approx}200~\mathrm{nm}$ , presents a large surface area $(1203\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ (Figure 1a and S1, S2). Upon pyrolysis at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the obtained $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ with high $Z\mathrm{n}$ loading of $6.64\mathrm{wt\%}$ shows well retained morphology as the parent ZIF-8 (Figure 1b and c). No obvious $Z\mathrm{n}$ or $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticle is observable, indicating the possible atomic dispersion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ species on the carbon skeleton of $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ (Figure 1c and S3). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) indicates the existence of $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ species in $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ without metallic $Z\mathrm{n}$ (Figure S4). After the removal of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms in $Z\mathrm{n}$ - $\bf N_{3}{-}C$ via HCl etching, followed by refilling extrinsic $\mathrm{Ni}$ atoms, the $\mathrm{Ni}{\bf-N}_{3}.$ -C catalyst with $0.85\mathrm{wt}\%$ Ni loading can be obtained. Both SEM and TEM results show that the particle size and polyhedral morphology of $\mathrm{\bfNi-N_{3}-C}$ are almost identical to $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ (Figure 1 d and e). No metallic Ni can be identified in the TEM image of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ , implying the possible atomic dispersion of Ni species (Figure 1 e). Nitrogen sorption measurements reveal a high surface area $(821{\bf m}^{2}{\bf g}^{-1},$ ) of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}-C}$ with similar pore size to that of $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ , manifesting the retained pore structure after the PSMS process (Figure S5).
Powder $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ presents two broaden peaks corresponding to the (002) and (101) planes of carbon and no Ni-containing crystalline phases can be observed (Figure S6), in accordance with the above TEM results. Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{3}.$ -C shows a low intensity ratio of D band to G band, which is similar to $Z_{\mathrm{n-N}_{3}}$ - C, indicating the high graphitization degree of $\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{N}_{3}{-}($ C (Figure S7). In addition to the characterizations on the microstructure, the chemical composition and electronic state of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}-C}$ have been further investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The Ni2p spectrum of Ni$\bf N_{3}{-}C$ indicates the Ni oxidation state locating between $\mathbf{N}\mathbf{\dot{i}}^{0}$ $(853.0\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ $(855.7\mathrm{eV})$ , suggesting the existence of partially oxidized Ni species (Figure S8).[10] The N 1s spectrum can be fitted into five typical peaks corresponding to pyridinic $\mathsf{V}(398.3\mathrm{eV})$ , metal-N species $(399.1\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic N $(400.2\mathrm{eV})$ , graphitic N $(401.2\mathrm{eV})$ and oxidized N $(402.9\mathrm{eV})$ , respectively (Figure 2 a).[10] The presence of oxidized Ni and metal-N species in ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ -C supports the atomic dispersion of Ni in the form of Ni-N coordination (Figure 2a and S8). Furthermore, the bright dots in the aberration-corrected highangle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ clearly display the atomic dispersion of Ni atoms (Figure 1 f). The results above clearly confirm the successful construction of single-atom Ni sites in ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ -C after post-synthetic substitution of $Z\mathrm{n}$ by Ni atoms.
To further investigate the controlled formation process from $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}}–\mathrm{C}$ at atomic scale, the synchrotronbased X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) measurements have been performed on $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}{\bf-N}_{3}.$ -C. The Zn Kedge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectrum of $Z\mathrm{n-N}_{3}.$ -C shows a higher near-edge absorption energy than $Z\mathrm{n}$ foil, manifesting that $Z\mathrm{n}$ is in an oxidized state in $Z\mathrm{n}$ - $\bf N_{3}{-}C_{:}$ , in line with the XPS results (Figure S9a). In addition, the Zn K-edge Fourier transforms (FT) extended $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) shows the atomic dispersion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms without $Z\mathrm{n-}Z\mathrm{n}$ bonding (Figure S9b). Further EXAFS fitting reveals that each $Z\mathrm{n}$ atom is coordinated by $3\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}$ atoms, confirming that $\mathrm{Zn-N}_{3}$ is the dominant coordination mode in $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ (Figure S10, Table S1). After HCl treatment, $\approx85\%$ Zn atoms can be removed from $Z_{\mathrm{{n-N_{3}-C}}}$ , leading to $\bf{N-C}$ with abundant $Z\mathrm{n}$ vacancy (Figure S11). By virtue of the vacancy sites in the NC, Ni atoms are introduced to afford Ni-N3-C. As expected, the Ni atoms in the resulting $N i{\mathrm{-}}N_{3^{-}}C$ present similar oxidation state and coordination environment to those of $Z\mathrm{n}$ in $\mathbf{\calZ}\mathbf{n}{\cdot}\mathbf{N}_{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ . Specifically, in the Ni K-edge XANES spectrum, the near-edge absorption energy of ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathbf{N_{3}-C}$ locates between Ni foil and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , indicating the oxidized Ni species in $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ (Figure 2b). In addition, the Ni K-edge FTEXAFS of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ displays a dominated peak centered at $1.43\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ corresponding to the Ni-N scattering path, and no peaks for Ni-Ni path can be observed (Figure 2c), which clearly suggests the formation of single-atom Ni species. Furthermore, the EXAFS fitting for $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}}–\mathrm{C}$ indicates that each $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ atom is 3-coordinated by three nitrogen atoms, similar to that of $Z\mathrm{n}$ in $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ (Figure 2d and S12, Table S2). The high degree of consistency of the oxidation state and coordination number between the single Zn $\mathbf{\mathrm{Zn}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}_{3}\mathbf{-}$ C) and Ni $(\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–\mathrm{C})$ atoms perfectly demonstrates the reliability of the PSMS strategy for the rational control of coordination environment.
In light of the characterization results above, the formation process of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ by PSMS approach can be well understood. Due to the low boiling point $(1180\mathrm{K})$ of metallic $Z\mathrm{n}$ , the generated $Z\mathrm{nNPs}$ at high temperature could readily evaporate away in the pyrolysis process of ZIF-8, leaving behind single-atom $Z\mathrm{n}$ species coordinated by N atoms in the porous carbon matrix.[11] Such unique “self-clean” mechanism of $Z\mathrm{n}$ element makes it much easier to obtain single-atom $Z\mathrm{n}$ at elevated temperature without $Z\mathrm{n}$ particles. Meanwhile, the N coordination number of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms can be finely regulated by controlling pyrolysis temperatures. When the pyrolysis temperature of ZIF-8 reaches $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $\mathbf{N}$ coordination number of single $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms can be controlled at 3, affording $Z\mathrm{n-N}_{3}$ -C featuring $\mathrm{Zn-N}_{3}$ sites (Figure S9 and S10). Once the single $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms are removed, abundant $Z\mathrm{n}$ vacancies with relatively high energy, which, surrounding by $\mathbf{N}$ atoms with lone-pair electrons, are readily occupied by extrinsic metal atoms with empty d orbital by coordination bonding, lowering the energy of the system. In this situation, when Ni atoms are introduced, they are prone to refill into the $Z\mathrm{n}$ vacancies spontaneously, thereby producing ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ -C with abundant Ni${\bf N}_{3}$ sites (Figure 2 b–d). To further manifest the universality of such PSMS strategy, $Z_{\mathrm{{n-N_{4}}}}$ -C, with similar physicochemical properties to $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{3}-C}}$ , has also been constructed at a slightly lower pyrolysis temperature $(800^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , guaranteeing sufficient
N content for the formation of $\mathrm{{Zn-N_{4}}}$ sites (Figure S6, S7, S13 and S14, Table S1). Upon acid etching and subsequent Ni decoration, the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ -C involving $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ sites is constructed (Figure S15, Table S2). To our delight, $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}{\mathrm{-C}}}$ exhibits similar Ni loading $(1.06\mathrm{wt\%})$ and physicochemical properties to Ni- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{N_{3}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}$ (Figure S5, S6 and S14). Furthermore, Fe- ${\bf\cdot N}_{3}$ -C and $\mathrm{Co-N}_{3}–\mathrm{C}$ , with the central atoms coordinated by $3\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}$ atoms, are also successfully fabricated by PSMS process similar to $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ (Figure S16 and S17, Table S3). These results unambiguously manifest the inheritance of both coordination number and microstructure from $Z\mathrm{n-N}_{x}–C$ to Fe-, Co-, and $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{x}$ -C, highlighting the promising universality of PSMS strategy toward the fabrication of SACs with wellcontrolled coordination number. It can be seen that the core idea of PSMS strategy is the predesign of single-atom $Z\mathrm{n}$ catalysts with well-controlled coordination number followed by the exterior metal substitution to inherit the coordination number of single $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms; this provides a new way to the accurate coordination number modulation of SACs.
Encouraged by the results above, electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction over $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{x}–\mathrm{C}$ has been examined. As indicated by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) profiles, $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}-C}$ presents much larger current response for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction than that of Ni- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}-C}$ and N-C, suggesting the higher activity of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ (Figure 3a, S18–S20). Significantly, $\mathrm{Ni}{\bf-N}_{3}.$ C shows a maximum CO FE up to $95.6\%$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V},$ far surpassing that of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}-C}$ $(89.2\%$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V},$ ), N–C ( $76.1\%$ at $-0.55\mathrm{V}.$ ), $Z\mathrm{n}$ - $\bf N_{3}{-}C$ $59.5\%$ at $-0.60\mathrm{V})$ and $Z_{\mathrm{n-N_{4}-C}}$ $(56.7\%$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V})$ (Figure $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ and S21). Meanwhile, the CO FE of $\mathrm{Ni}{\bf-N}_{3}.$ -C can be maintained above $90\%$ in a wide potential window from $-0.55\mathrm{V}$ to $-0.9{\mathrm{V}}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as the only byproduct (Figure 3b, S22 and S23). Furthermore, ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ -C shows a CO partial current density $(J_{\mathrm{CO}})$ of $6.64\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V}$ with a TOF of $1425{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ , much higher than those of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}-C}$ and N–C (Figure 3c and S24). The Tafel slope of Ni- ${\bf\cdot N}_{3}$ -C is determined to be $99\mathrm{mV}_{I}$ /decade, smaller than that of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}\mathrm{-C}}$ and N-C, indicating the much favorable kinetics of $\mathrm{Ni}{\bf-N}_{3}$ -C for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ (Figure 3d). Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ also shows smaller charge transfer resistance than $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}\mathrm{-C}}$ , further supporting its much higher catalytic activity (Figure S25). In addition, the bestperformed $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}{\mathrm{-}}($ also shows ultrahigh stability with nearly unchanged current density and CO FE under $-0.65\mathrm{V}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 3 e). These results unambiguously manifest that Ni$\mathrm{N}_{3}–\mathrm{C},$ with lower coordination number of N, exhibits superior catalytic performance for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{R}\mathbf{R}$ , highlighting the critical role of coordination microenvironment of single-atom Ni sites toward electrocatalysis.
In view of the excellent catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ toward $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ , the $\scriptstyle{\mathbf{Z}}{\mathrm{n-CO}}_{2}$ battery, as a promising energy conversion technique for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ utilization,[13] has been investigated by employing $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ as a cathode catalyst (Figure 4a). In the discharge and charge voltages under different current densities, $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ shows a discharge potential of $0.41\mathrm{V}$ at $0.5\mathrm{mA}$ during discharge process (Figure 4 b).
Encouragingly, when the discharge current reaches $2.5\mathrm{mA}$ , the discharge potential can be maintained at $0.2\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 4b). Meanwhile, as an anode in the battery, ${\bf N i-N}_{3}$ -C displays a charge potential of $2.4\mathrm{V}$ at $0.5\mathrm{mA}$ , which reaches a relatively stable state after $1\mathrm{mA}$ (Figure 4b). Moreover, the FE of CO in the discharge process reaches a maximum value of $93\%$ at $2\mathrm{mA}$ , which can be maintained in a wide discharge current range, manifesting the excellent selectivity to CO of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ in $Z\mathrm{n-CO}_{2}$ battery (Figure 4c). In addition, the consecutive discharge-charge cycles at $2\mathrm{mA}$ present the stable voltage of $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ after 100 cycles, underscoring the excellent durability of the cell (Figure 4 d).
In conclusion, a facile and rational PSMS strategy has been developed to fabricate single-atom Ni catalysts by replacing the atomically dispersed $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites with Ni atoms in $Z\mathrm{n-N}_{x}–C$ derived from ZIF-8. With this PSMS strategy, the synthesis of SACs from MOF is divided into multi steps yet simplifies the parameters in each step, making the synthesis much more general, controllable and straightforward. Thanks to the unique “self-clean” behavior of the low-boiling metallic Zn during pyrolysis, the single-atom Zn can be readily produced in the absence of concomitant $Z\mathrm{n}$ particle. Strikingly, the coordination number of single $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms in $Z\mathrm{n-N_{x}-C}$ can be easily regulated by simply varying the pyrolysis temperature. Once extrinsic $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ atoms are introduced to substitute the single-atom $Z\mathrm{n}$ , not only their atomic dispersion but also coordination configuration can be inherited in a well-controlled manner. Remarkably, the optimal $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ , with single-atom Ni coordinated by $3\mathrm{N}$ atoms, displays excellent performance for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction with ultrahigh CO FE $(95.6\%)$ and TOF value $(1425\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V},$ much higher than those of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}{\mathrm{-C}}$ with $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ sites. Theoretical calculations unveil that the lower Ni coordination number in $\mathrm{Ni-N_{3}}–C$ greatly reduces the formation energy of the rate determining step, thereby promoting the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction process. In addition, the assembled $Z_{\mathrm{{n-CO}_{2}}}$ battery with $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{3}–C$ as a cathode catalyst offers ultrahigh FE of CO in the discharge process with excellent stability. This work not only demonstrates a facile, novel, general and rationally controlled strategy to manipulate the coordination microenvironment of SACs but also lays a foundation for the promising application of SACs in rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n-CO}_{2}$ batteries.
